contentText:
Phoebe Bridgers &#8216;&nbsp;last album,&nbsp; Punisher , arrived&nbsp;in June 2020, in the early, uncertain days of COVID-19.&nbsp;Though it was written prior to the pandemic, the&nbsp;album&nbsp;became a perfect&nbsp;lockdown&nbsp;summer soundtrack:&nbsp;Sardonic,&nbsp;reflective&nbsp;and&nbsp;restless,&nbsp;it unintentionally captured the mood&nbsp;of the time,&nbsp;particularly with&nbsp;a scream into the void on closer&nbsp;“I Know the End.”&nbsp;

Bridgers&nbsp;couldn’t&nbsp;tour&nbsp; Punisher &nbsp;upon its release,&nbsp;so instead she hosted a&nbsp;handful&nbsp;of livestreams from her apartment for what she dubbed her World Tour&nbsp;in 2020.&nbsp;As restrictions lifted in 2021, she more than made up for lost time with the Reunion Tour, which kicked off in September 2021&nbsp;and ended in April 2023, just in time to start up&nbsp;a tour with&nbsp;boygenius&nbsp;— her supergroup with Julien Baker and Lucy Dacus&nbsp;—&nbsp;&nbsp;that&nbsp;went into 2024&nbsp;and, on her own,&nbsp;join&nbsp;Taylor Swift&#8217;s&nbsp;Eras Tour&nbsp;for a&nbsp;dozen&nbsp;dates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

The spacious and experimental&nbsp; Lost Weekend , out today (Aug. 14) on Dead Oceans, was written over a period of both loss and love.&nbsp;A lot has happened&nbsp;for&nbsp;the Los Angeles singer-songwriter since&nbsp;2020, from&nbsp;launching&nbsp;her own Saddest Factory Records&nbsp;(which has since merged with Dead Oceans) to&nbsp;getting back on the road&nbsp;and releasing the debut&nbsp;boygenius&nbsp;album&nbsp;to&nbsp;a&nbsp;high-profile breakup with actor Paul&nbsp;Mescal,&nbsp;the death of her father&nbsp;and the start of a new relationship with partner (and now credited collaborator) Bo Burnham.&nbsp;&nbsp;

With these events swirling in the background, Bridgers takes on an edgier&nbsp;tone on&nbsp; Lost Weekend.&nbsp; The album&nbsp;fluctuates between grief and elation,&nbsp;agitation&nbsp;and contentment, as Bridgers&nbsp;sings&nbsp;like someone&nbsp;who has&nbsp;loved and&nbsp;lost&nbsp;—&nbsp;and still has&nbsp;something to lose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Lost&nbsp;Weekend ’s&nbsp;extensive&nbsp;production&nbsp;credits include&nbsp;Bridgers,&nbsp;Tony&nbsp;Berg&nbsp;and Ethan Gruska, who both worked on&nbsp; Punisher&nbsp; and Bridgers’ debut&nbsp;studio album,&nbsp; Stranger in the Alps; &nbsp;and&nbsp;Jack Antonoff, with&nbsp;additional&nbsp;production from Alex G.&nbsp;Some songs&nbsp;function&nbsp;as interstitial,&nbsp;ambient-leaning transitions, while others are meant to stand on their own.&nbsp;Over an ambitious 16 tracks,&nbsp;Bridgers&nbsp;has&nbsp;room to wander, which she does with some&nbsp;country flair and&nbsp;new&nbsp;electronic touches. But while the album may&nbsp;turn&nbsp;some new heads, OG Phoebe fans need not&nbsp;worry:&nbsp;The intricate,&nbsp;scene-setting lyrics, the delicate yet powerful voice and the&nbsp;gallows&nbsp;humor&nbsp;all&nbsp;remain.&nbsp;

